And then there's a thousand-year-old secret.
This is the story of a man from Turkey who made a big discovery while repairing the house.
When the wall of his basement was broken while repairing, it collided with a secret door.
From here he reached an underground tunnel, where he was confronted with a thousand-year-old city hidden within the ground.
Pictures of this city show how about 20,000 people used to live in this under-gread city with all their belongings and arrangements for all their food items.
600 Gates to Enter the City:
The incident took place in 1963 when a Turkish man living in Kappadosia discovered the city of Under-Green.
The wall of his basement was smashed while repairing the house.
From here found an under-ground city named Darinyuku present in a depth of 280 feet inside the ground.
Its depth was equal to an 18-storey building.
This underground city was sometimes home to 20,000 people, ranging from rooms, coaches, schools, churches, toccupies, etc. to ventilation.
There are 600 gates to enter this city and get ether from here.
There are heavy doors installed here, which are meant to prevent infiltrators in the underground city.
Each floor can be folded separately.
It is believed that this city was built between 780 and 11800 ESVI during Bijanteen so that people here could be protected in wars and natural calamities between the Arabs and Zonians.
It's become a tourist attraction:
Derinkuyu is not the only underground city in the world.
There are many other such cities, but it is the largest underground city.
Only half of this city can be reached, but this place has been converted into a popular tourist attraction in Kappadosia.
Due to its historical and cultural importance, people come here to see it.
