U.S.-U.S. is responsible for cyber-attacks in the world
Western countries have accused Russia's military intelligence department of carrying out cyberattacks all over the world.
The United States, the United Kingdom and the Netherlands have accused Russia of carrying out cyberattacks on some important institutions.
The U.S. Ministry of Defense (USA) has registered a case of cyber-attacking conspiracy against seven Russian-allegants.
The U.S. claims that FIFA, World Anti-Doping Agency and a U.S. nuclear-armed company was at their target.
All of them are also accused of trying to hack a computer from the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapon (OPCW).
This organization is investigating the alleged Nerve attack on a former Russian spy in the UK.
Russia, however, has denied all the allegations.
Allegies against Russia
Netherland has accused four Russian citizens of trying to hack into the OPCW organization.
The United Kingdom has accused Russia's intelligence department behind four high-profile cyber attacks.
According to the United Kingdom, this includes several companies from Russia and Ukraine, the Democratic Party of the United States and a small TV network in Britain.
In addition, the United States has alleged that Russia's intelligence agencies carried out cyberattacks on U.S. anti-doping agency and nuclear-energy company "Wasting House."
Canada has alleged that Russia's intelligence agency carried out cyberattacks on its "Center for Ethics in Sports and the World Anti-Doping Agency."
Moreover, the Dutch government alleges that it seized a laptop with four Russian suspects in April.
It was also used in Brazil, Switzerland and Malaysia.
According to the Netherlands, this cyberattack in Malaysia was carried out to influence the MH17 flight-related investigation.
The aircraft was shot down in a Russian-baccuditive area in Ukraine.
A total of 298 people on board were killed.
"What did Russia say?
Russia's Foreign Ministry refutes allegations against Britain and the Netherlands.
In an official statement, he said, "Russia has become a victim of misinformation in a planned manner.
Russia has said that any person in Russia who owns a mobile phone has been described as a spy.
Four out of the seven U.S. people have been expelled from the Netherlands for cyber-attacks.
At the same time, three people are an accused in connection with the hacking of Democratic officials in the U.S. elections.
He was also accused of cheating, concealment of identity and money laundering.
Britain's Prime Minister and the Dutch Prime Minister said in a joint statement that Russia's espionage organization has ignored global values and rules by conspiring the alleged cyberattack against OPCW.
Meanwhile, Britain's Foreign Secretary Germy Hunt has said that Britain along with its allies is considering imposing further sanctions on Russia.
The United Nations has also criticized Russia in this matter.
